Scaling: memcpy 3155 -> 5094 MB/s (1.61x on 4 threads), XOR 2889 -> 4156 MB/s (1.44x).
Far below 4x -- this is a single-channel-DDR Athlon with shared L2/L3, so the large-buffer
copy is memory-bandwidth bound, not core bound.
Encode rows match between the two runs within noise (they are single-threaded either way).
The multi-core run's decode rows came out 20-29% lower (k=2 1067->857, k=4 855->667, k=8 842->599),
while encode in the same run did not move. The host carried a neighbour load of ~1.0 throughout,
and the multi-core phase had just saturated all four cores with the 4-thread copy, so the decode
drop is a contention artefact of that run, not a real regression: treat the single-core run as
canonical for RLNC.
Encode is flat in W at fixed d (d=8: ~1302/1322/1325 across W=8/16/32), and drops as d grows
(d=16: ~646) -- cost tracks d (the number of XOR-accumulated sources), not the generation width.
